<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Applications">Applications<=
/h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[=
</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DSTD_Bus&amp;=
action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D3" title=3D"Edit section: Applications"><span>e=
dit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>A focus of the STD bus was its ability to build a system using the exact=
 bus cards required for an application.  The compact size of a card made th=
e STD bus system more adaptable to various applications than the contempora=
ry computer buses of the mid-1980s such as the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/S-100_bus" title=3D"S-100 bus">S-100</a> and the <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS-50_bus" title=3D"SS-50 bus">SS-50</a>, becau=
se it could use <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Servo_control" tit=
le=3D"Servo control">servo control</a> cards along with a fully <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_programming" title=3D"Computer progr=
amming">programmable computer</a> for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Operation_(mathematics)" title=3D"Operation (mathematics)">mathematical=
 operations</a>.
</p><p>In applications for running an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Observatory" title=3D"Observatory">astronomical observatory</a>, the la=
rge industrial base of cards, and the system's expandability, made the syst=
em desirable for use in a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photomet=
ry_(optics)" title=3D"Photometry (optics)">photometry</a> lab to control th=
e <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telescope" title=3D"Telescope">t=
elescope</a> as well as do the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dat=
a_logging" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Data logging">data logging</a> an=
d computations required.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/STD_Bus#cite_note-2"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>2<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In typical university laboratory settings of the mid - late 80's, ST=
D bus data acquisition systems were commonplace using Z80 or similar proces=
sor cards for the data capture, processing and control, parallel I/O cards =
for experiment control as well as analogue to digital conversion cards for =
reading experiment analogue parameters. Such systems would only occupy mini=
mal rack space, while providing full <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/CP/M" title=3D"CP/M">CP/M</a> processing features.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-3"=
 class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/STD_Bus#cite_=
note-3"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>3<span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"STD-32">STD-32</h2><span cl=
ass=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DSTD_Bus&amp;action=3Dedi=
t&amp;section=3D4" title=3D"Edit section: STD-32"><span>edit</span></a><spa=
n class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>The STD-32 is a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pin_compatibili=
ty" title=3D"Pin compatibility">pin compatible</a> STD interface that allow=
s the co-existence of both 8-bit and 32-bit systems on a single bus. This i=
s accomplished by the addition of pins between the normal pins that do not =
connect, nor do they interfere with the original specification. This allows=
 with the proper STD-32 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backplane"=
 title=3D"Backplane">backplane</a> the ability to run <a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legacy_system" title=3D"Legacy system">legacy</a> cards=
 used for specific applications on the same bus without having to upgrade t=
he complete system.
</p>
